 "raw_reply": "{\n  \"experiment_id\": \"EXP-034\",\n  \"observed_evidence\": [\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"extra_trees (raw stage, baseline, 3 folds, 1 repeat) has mean ROC-AUC 1.0, std 0.0, and per-fold ROC-AUC of exactly 1.0 in all three folds.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-034 / evidence.model_results[1].metrics.roc_auc\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"The selection rule ranks by mean ROC-AUC minus 0.25×fold std, then runtime, on training CV only.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-034 / evidence.selection_rule\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"The other tree models are within about 1e-4 of extra_trees on mean ROC-AUC: hist_gradient_boosting 0.99994, lightgbm 0.99998, xgboost 0.99999.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-034 / evidence.model_results[2].metrics.roc_auc.mean; evidence.model_results[3].metrics.roc_auc.mean; evidence.model_results[4].metrics.roc_auc.mean\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Logistic regression has mean ROC-AUC 0.99918 and mean log loss 0.0479, the weakest of the five models.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-034 / evidence.model_results[0].metrics.roc_auc.mean; evidence.model_results[0].metrics.log_loss.mean\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"extra_trees, hist_gradient_boosting, lightgbm and xgboost have identical mean accuracy (0.99937), mean recall (0.99870) and mean F1 (0.99935). Fold 3 accuracy is 0.99812 and recall is 0.99611 for all four.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-034 / evidence.model_results[1].metrics.accuracy.mean; evidence.model_results[1].fold_metrics[2]; evidence.model_results[2].fold_metrics[2]; evidence.model_results[3].fold_metrics[2]; evidence.model_results[4].fold_metrics[2]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"extra_trees has fold-3 ROC-AUC 1.0 but fold-3 recall 0.99611 at threshold 0.5.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-034 / evidence.model_results[1].fold_metrics[2]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"extra_trees has the lowest mean log loss of the tree models: 0.00167, against xgboost 0.00374, hist_gradient_boosting 0.00579 and lightgbm 0.00634.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-034 / evidence.model_results[1].metrics.log_loss.mean; evidence.model_results[4].metrics.log_loss.mean; evidence.model_results[2].metrics.log_loss.mean; evidence.model_results[3].metrics.log_loss.mean\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"xgboost puts a mean normalized importance of about 0.866 on 'odor'. extra_trees spreads importance more evenly, with odor at about 0.140.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-034 / evidence.model_results[4].feature_stability[0]; evidence.model_results[1].feature_stability[0]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"All models use 3 folds and 1 repeat with default parameters (empty model_params, optimization 'baseline'), and each uses 22 features.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-034 / evidence.model_results[*].folds; evidence.model_results[*].repeats; evidence.model_results[*].model_params; evidence.model_results[*].feature_count_mean\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"hist_gradient_boosting reports no feature stability data.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-034 / evidence.model_results[2].feature_stability\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"The bundle's top-level limitations list is empty.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-034 / limitations\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"interpretation\": \"Inference: ROC-AUC is saturated for all five models, and the four tree ensembles are practically tied on training CV. extra_trees leads the stated rule only because it reaches exactly 1.0 with zero std, while the margins over the other models are about 1e-5 to 1e-4. With 3 folds and 1 repeat, that margin is not shown to be distinguishable from noise. The identical fold-3 accuracy and recall across the four tree models suggest, but do not prove, that the same hard example or examples are missed. That could be label noise, a duplicate-like record, or a boundary case, and it needs inspection. A perfect AUC alongside a recall miss at threshold 0.5 shows that ranking quality and the fixed decision threshold are separate questions. Feature importances describe model usage, not causal drivers, and the odor dominance in xgboost suggests the task may be near-separable on a few attributes. The evidence supports a provisional training-CV candidate, not 'strongest algorithm family'.\",\n  \"decision\": \"Retain extra_trees only as a provisional training-CV candidate, tied in practice with the boosted trees. Do not describe it as the strongest or a stable winner, do not claim generalization (the locked holdout is unseen), and do not approve for deployment or feature use. Next, run a repeated, duplicate-aware comparison before any holdout evaluation.\",\n  \"risks_and_missing_evidence\": [\n    \"Ceiling effect: ROC-AUC near 1.0 across models leaves the selection rule almost no discriminating power, so rank order may be determined by runtime or noise.\",\n    \"Only 3 folds and 1 repeat; the std of 0.0 is computed over 3 values and is not a reliable stability estimate.\",\n    \"The bundle gives no dataset row count, class balance, positive-class definition, or duplicate-row audit. Fold sizes implied by the metrics look small, but this is an inference and cannot be confirmed from the bundle. Duplicate or near-duplicate rows across folds could inflate CV scores, so group-aware splitting needs checking.\",\n    \"No decision-time or production-availability review of features such as odor, spore-print-color, gill-size and bruises. Some may be recorded only after consumption or lab inspection, or by an expert, so they may not exist at the real decision point. 22 features were used while the original dataset typically has 23; the bundle does not say which column was dropped or why.\",\n    \"Threshold fixed at 0.5 with no analysis of the asymmetric cost of false negatives versus false positives. Fold-3 recall of 0.996 for the top models suggests residual errors that matter if the positive class is the harmful one, but the bundle does not define the positive class.\",\n    \"All models are untuned baselines with empty model_params, so the comparison is between defaults and not between tuned algorithm families.\",\n    \"No preprocessing description (categorical encoding differs between linear and tree models), so effects of encoding on the comparison cannot be assessed. Pipeline fit inside folds is not documented.\",\n    \"Feature importances are model-specific, are not comparable across algorithms, and are empty for hist_gradient_boosting. They must not be read causally.\",\n    \"The empty limitations list in the bundle understates known caveats such as a single dataset profile and an unseen holdout.\"\n  ],\n  \"challenges_to_claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-034-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"The statement '1.0000±0.0000 ROC-AUC across folds' matches evidence.model_results[1].metrics.roc_auc, but it implies a stable, distinguishable lead. Competing models differ by only about 1e-5 to 1e-4 ROC-AUC, based on 3 folds and 1 repeat, and the zero std is not meaningful stability evidence.\",\n      \"severity\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-034-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"The claim does not state that extra_trees ties the boosted models on accuracy, recall and F1, with the same fold-3 error profile, or that it has a recall miss at threshold 0.5 despite perfect AUC. Selecting on ROC-AUC alone hides this.\",\n      \"severity\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-034-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"The claim's limitation covers the unseen holdout but omits unaudited duplicate rows, fold-size and sample-size uncertainty, and the absence of any decision-time or production-availability review of the raw features used.\",\n      \"severity\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-034-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"'Algorithm family' ranking is based on default hyperparameters only. The lead of one family over others cannot be attributed to the family rather than to default settings.\",\n      \"severity\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"next_experiment\": {\n    \"title\": \"Repeated, duplicate-grouped CV tie-break among extra_trees, xgboost, lightgbm and hist_gradient_boosting\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"If extra_trees is genuinely better than the boosted trees, it will keep the best mean log loss and a lower or equal error count across repeated duplicate-grouped CV. If the lead is a ceiling artifact, paired differences will include zero and ranks will change between repeats.\",\n    \"success_gate\": \"Pre-registered: run 5x repeated stratified 5-fold CV with identical splits for all four models, with rows grouped by identical feature vectors so duplicates never straddle folds. Report ROC-AUC, log loss and error count at threshold 0.5, plus the paired mean difference with a 95% interval versus the best competitor. extra_trees keeps its candidate status only if it is first on mean log loss in at least 80% of repeats and the paired log-loss interval versus the best competitor excludes zero. Otherwise record the models as statistically tied and choose by a pre-declared secondary criterion (error count, then runtime). Also list the specific fold-3 misclassified rows and their duplicate or label status. Decision-time availability review of odor, spore-print-color, gill-size and bruises is a prerequisite before any feature is endorsed.\",\n    \"why_smallest\": \"It reuses the existing raw features and default models, adds only repeats and group-aware splits, and tests the single weak point, which is that the ROC-AUC ceiling and 3-fold design cannot separate the models. It involves no tuning, no new features and no holdout access.\"\n  },\n  \"overall_confidence\": \"medium\"\n}",
